---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/aspe/aspe3290.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "THE THREE-CATEGORY LIKELIHOOD FRAMEWORK" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Contingent Losses — Accrual Criteria" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Contingent Losses — Likely but Not Reasonably Estimable" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Contingent Losses — Unlikely or Not Determinable" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Contingent Gains — Never Accrued" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Terminology — \"Contingency,\" Not \"Provision\"" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example — Accruing a Likely, Estimable Loss Contingency" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Worked Example — Disclosing an Unlikely Contingent Gain" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Relationship to IAS 37" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s16", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">
</div>





<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Accounting for genuinely uncertain future events</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 3290 governs the RECOGNITION and DISCLOSURE of CONTINGENCIES — EXISTING conditions OR situations INVOLVING uncertainty AS to a POSSIBLE gain OR loss, TO be resolved BY a FUTURE event. GENUINELY distinctively, ASPE retains "CONTINGENCY" terminology THROUGHOUT (Section 8), and USES a THREE-category likelihood FRAMEWORK — LIKELY, unlikely, AND not determinable — RATHER than IAS 37'S own PROBABLE/possible/remote STRUCTURE (Section 3).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Uncertain future events, both losses and gains</div><div class="principle">This Section applies TO contingent LOSSES and CONTINGENT gains — EXCLUDING asset RETIREMENT obligations (SECTION 3110), WHICH involve a KNOWN legal OBLIGATION rather THAN a genuinely UNCERTAIN future EVENT.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>THE THREE-CATEGORY LIKELIHOOD FRAMEWORK</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Likely, unlikely, and not determinable</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Category</span><span>Description</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Likely</span><span>THE future EVENT confirming the LOSS or GAIN is LIKELY to OCCUR</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Unlikely</span><span>THE future EVENT is UNLIKELY to OCCUR</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Not determinable</span><span>THE likelihood CANNOT reasonably BE determined GIVEN the AVAILABLE information</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Contingent Losses — Accrual Criteria</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Likely, and reasonably estimable</div><div class="text-supporting">A CONTINGENT loss IS accrued (RECOGNIZED as a LIABILITY and EXPENSE) WHERE it IS BOTH LIKELY that a FUTURE event WILL confirm THE loss, AND the AMOUNT can BE reasonably ESTIMATED — BOTH conditions MUST be MET together FOR accrual to BE appropriate.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Contingent Losses — Likely but Not Reasonably Estimable</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Disclosed, not accrued</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE a LOSS is LIKELY but CANNOT be REASONABLY estimated, IT is DISCLOSED (DESCRIBING the NATURE of the CONTINGENCY and, WHERE possible, a RANGE of POTENTIAL loss), BUT NOT accrued as a LIABILITY.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Contingent Losses — Unlikely or Not Determinable</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Generally no disclosure required, with limited exceptions</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE a LOSS is UNLIKELY, NO disclosure IS generally REQUIRED (THOUGH an ENTERPRISE may VOLUNTARILY disclose IT). WHERE likelihood IS not DETERMINABLE, DISCLOSURE is GENERALLY required, GIVEN the GENUINE uncertainty INVOLVED could STILL be MATERIAL to a READER'S understanding.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Contingent Gains — Never Accrued</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A conservative, asymmetric treatment</div><div class="text-supporting">CONTINGENT gains are NEVER accrued PRIOR to REALIZATION, REGARDLESS of HOW likely THEY may APPEAR — accruing an UNREALIZED gain COULD result IN recognizing INCOME that MAY never ACTUALLY be REALIZED, a RESULT the CONSERVATISM principle (RETAINED explicitly WITHIN Section 1000'S own QUALITATIVE characteristics — see the COMPANION poster) SPECIFICALLY guards AGAINST. A CONTINGENT gain IS disclosed only WHERE it IS likely TO be realized, AVOIDING misleading IMPLICATIONS of confidence.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Terminology — "Contingency," Not "Provision"</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A single, unified framework rather than a split provisions/contingencies structure</div><div class="text-supporting">INTERNATIONALLY, IAS 37 explicitly DISTINGUISHES between "PROVISIONS" (liabilities OF uncertain TIMING or AMOUNT that ARE nonetheless RECOGNIZED, GIVEN a PROBABLE outflow) and "CONTINGENCIES" (matters TOO uncertain TO recognize, ONLY disclosed). ASPE, BY contrast, RETAINS a SINGLE, unified "CONTINGENCY" framework THROUGHOUT — WHAT IFRS WOULD call a "PROVISION" is SIMPLY an ACCRUED contingent LOSS under ASPE'S OWN terminology, RATHER than a SEPARATELY labeled CONCEPT. THIS is a GENUINELY notable TERMINOLOGICAL divergence, EVEN where the UNDERLYING accrual OUTCOMES are LARGELY similar IN substance.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Accruing a Likely, Estimable Loss Contingency</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Both criteria genuinely met</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A pending lawsuit with a probable, estimable outcome</div><div class="wk-body">A company is a defendant in a lawsuit. Legal counsel advises that an unfavorable outcome is likely, and estimates the probable loss at $350,000, based on comparable settled cases.</div><div class="wk-answer">GIVEN the LOSS is BOTH LIKELY and REASONABLY estimable, the COMPANY accrues a <strong>$350,000</strong> liability AND corresponding EXPENSE — CONSISTENT with the ACCRUAL criteria UNDER Section 3290.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — Disclosing an Unlikely Contingent Gain</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">No accrual for gains, regardless of likelihood</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A pending favorable litigation outcome</div><div class="wk-body">A company is the plaintiff in a lawsuit against a supplier, with legal counsel advising that a favorable outcome, resulting in a $500,000 award, is likely.</div><div class="wk-answer">DESPITE the FAVORABLE outcome BEING LIKELY, the COMPANY does <strong>NOT accrue</strong> the $500,000 GAIN — it MAY disclose the CONTINGENCY, GIVEN it IS likely TO be REALIZED, but RECOGNIZES no AMOUNT within the FINANCIAL statements UNTIL the GAIN is ACTUALLY realized.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 3290 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Nature of a contingent loss that is likely but not reasonably estimable, or where likelihood is not determinable</span><span>Nature of a contingent gain likely to be realized</span><span>Amount accrued for a contingent loss meeting the accrual criteria</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Classifying a contingency's likelihood as likely, unlikely, or not determinable</span><span>Whether a likely loss can be reasonably estimated</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Amount of a probable and estimable loss contingency requiring accrual</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Relationship to IAS 37</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASPE Section 3290 vs IAS 37</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 3290</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 37</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Terminology</span><span>Unified "contingency" framework</span><span>Separate "provisions" and "contingencies" concepts</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Likelihood categories</span><span>Likely / unlikely / not determinable</span><span>Probable / possible / remote</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">LARGELY converged IN substantive OUTCOME (LIKELY, estimable LOSSES are ACCRUED under BOTH frameworks), but a GENUINELY distinctive TERMINOLOGICAL and CLASSIFICATION structure THROUGHOUT.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every contingency event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Accrued loss contingency</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Liability and expense recognized</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Contingent gain</span><span>Notes only</span><span>Never accrued prior to realization</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1000</span><span>Financial Statement Concepts — supplies the conservatism principle underlying this Section's asymmetric loss/gain treatment</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3280</span><span>Contractual Obligations — governs known, existing commitments, distinct from this Section's uncertain future events</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Contingent loss</div><div class="glossary-def">A possible loss dependent on a future event, accrued if likely and reasonably estimable, otherwise disclosed or omitted depending on likelihood.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Contingent gain</div><div class="glossary-def">A possible gain dependent on a future event, never accrued prior to realization, disclosed only if likely.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe3290 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
